{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
 "company": "Orrin Fieldworks, Inc.",
 "ticker": null,
 "type": "private",
 "decision": "Whether Meridian Capital Ventures should lead Orrin Fieldworks' $18M Series A with a $12M check at a $72M pre-money valuation and take the Series A board seat",
 "language": "en"
}"}],"isError":false}

{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"research_plan (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "spines": [
  {
   "spine": "Deep tech / hard tech",
   "must_answer": "What has actually been proven about the Orrin Picker under field conditions, and what remains unproven? Specifically: whether a 94% pick rate measured on 212 rows at 9 of 14 pilot farms, chosen by the company, excluding tunnels, standing water and slopes over 4%, transfers to commercial blocks; whether 3.8% bruising is inside the retail tolerance that one lost pilot already tested; whether the IP is enforceable given that every claim of the lead gripper application was rejected in a first office action and the two core perception families are licensed, not owned, on a 100-robot exclusivity test due 2027-12-31."
  },
  {
   "spine": "Industrial / hardware",
   "must_answer": "Can Orrin build, deploy and service 360 robots for the 2027 season at a cost that earns a return? The test is the v4 BOM ($31,000 target, $33,000 assumed in the use-of-funds budget, $36,000 milestone ceiling against a $48,000 v3 build), the $16,500 2027 list price against $9,500 on signed 2025-2026 contracts, service cost of $3,100 plus $2,500 transport/insurance/consumables per robot-season, 86% uptime and one technician per 8 robots at fleet scale — and whether a 7-month California plus 4-month Florida year can keep a fleet utilised."
  },
  {
   "spine": "Early-stage startup",
   "must_answer": "Is there enough evidence to underwrite this team and its learning speed at a $72M pre-money? The test is whether a first-time CEO with a robotics PhD and a harvest-operations COO can convert 14 pilots into paid renewals (2 converted in 2025, 5 paying growers in 2026, one Florida pilot lost to bruising), hire a CTO after the co-founder CTO left in 2023-08, and reach the 2027 milestones (360 robots, $8M ARR on a definition that includes letters of intent and an engineering contract, unit cost below $36,000) on the $18M raise that carries 9 months of runway at $410,000 monthly net burn."
  }
 ],
 "industry_map": "Value chain: strawberry genetics and nursery stock (e.g. UC Davis and proprietary breeder programmes) -> growers (California and Florida, top-20 growers farm over half of California acreage per the deck) -> harvest execution (hand crews, farm-labour contractors, H-2A crews) and harvest aids (conveyor platforms, carts) -> robotic picking hardware, perception software and fleet service -> cooling/pack (clamshells, pallet) -> retail and foodservice. Decisive bottleneck: the peak-harvest labour window. Berries must be picked every three to four days for up to seven months, the crop is perishable and mould spreads from unharvested fruit, so a crew shortfall costs more than the fruit lost; that is what a robot must be priced against. Second bottleneck: capital and service intensity — the robot earns only within a roughly seven-month California season and a four-month Florida season against a $48,000 build, so utilisation across geographies and seasons, not pick rate alone, determines the return. Orrin's place: it is not a grower or a labour contractor but an equipment OEM plus a service operator — Orrin Fieldworks, Inc. holds the patents and the Calder University licence, Orrin Fleet Services LLC owns the fleet, signs the grower contracts and carries the Kestrel debt, so the capital intensity sits one layer below the parent that raises the equity. It competes for the grower's harvest budget against hand crews and $10,000-$30,000 harvest-aid platforms, not only against other robots.",
 "geo_lens": "Material. Map: (1) revenue markets — California (Watsonville, Salinas, Santa Maria) and Florida (Plant City) strawberry districts, different season calendars (CA roughly April-October, FL December-March) and different field systems (open-field vs. tabletop/tunnel); (2) the labour-supply market that sets the customer's willingness to pay — US H-2A guest-worker inflows, California overtime rules and Adverse Effect Wage Rates in the two states; (3) the supplier/manufacturing market — Jalisco, Mexico assembly (7 staff), USMCA rules of origin and US tariff exposure on Mexican-built capital goods, plus the contract manufacturer's jurisdiction; (4) the comparable-company market — US, European and Israeli harvesting-robotics vendors and the venture/strategic capital that funds them; (5) the exit market — US and European agricultural-machinery strategic buyers (Deere, CNH, AGCO, Kubota, Trimble) and the US agtech venture secondaries. Investor perspective is a US venture fund, so the underwriting currency is USD and the exit venue is a US/European strategic or IPO path.",
 "policy_lens": "Material. Jurisdictions to map: United States federal — H-2A temporary agricultural worker programme (adverse effect wage rates, 2025-2026 rule changes), USDA SBIR funding and USDA strawberry/berry research budgets, US tariffs and USMCA rules of origin for capital goods assembled in Mexico; California — Agricultural Labor Relations Act coverage, overtime and heat-illness standards that raise the cost of hand crews, and any automation or displacement policy; Florida — state guest-worker and labour-contractor licensing rules. Also relevant: university technology-transfer policy because the two core patent families are licensed from Calder University on a milestone-linked exclusivity, and patent-office procedure because the lead gripper application has had every claim rejected once. The policy channel here is mostly indirect but decision-relevant: any expansion of the legal guest-worker supply lowers the grower's cost of hand labour and therefore the robot's price ceiling, while tighter labour enforcement raises it.",
 "regulatory_databases": [
  "USPTO Patent Center and Patent Public Search — 5 own filings (US 17/902,118; US 18/655,430; US 19/204,876; US 63/811,204) plus prosecution status and the two licensed Calder families",
  "WIPO PATENTSCOPE / PCT — PCT/US2025/027761 national-phase deadline 2026-10-30 and designated states",
  "USPTO Assignments on the Web — confirm Calder University ownership of CU-2017-031 and CU-2019-114 and check for any encumbrance or later assignment",
  "SBIR.gov and USAspending.gov — USDA SBIR Phase II award ($650,000, 2024-2026) and any other federal obligations",
  "US DOL Office of Foreign Labor Certification disclosure data — H-2A certifications and adverse effect wage rates for California and Florida strawberry counties",
  "Delaware Division of Corporations and California Secretary of State — entity status, officers and any liens (Orrin Fieldworks, Inc.; Orrin Fleet Services LLC)",
  "UCC filings (California Secretary of State) — Kestrel Equipment Finance security interest in the fleet",
  "Mexico Registro Público de Comercio / SAT and the DOF foreign-investment registry — Orrin Fieldworks Mexico S. de R.L. de C.V. status and IMMEX/import permits",
  "Cal/OSHA and California Agricultural Labor Relations Board — heat, field-sanitation and labour-relations records relevant to hand-crew cost",
  "USDA NASS Quick Stats — California and Florida strawberry acreage, production value and grower prices to test the deck's acreage and market-size claims"
 ],
 "gates": [],
 "hypothesis": "The customer pain is real and independently documented, but at the deck's own numbers the current unit economics do not work: a $48,000 v3 robot depreciated over the stated 4 seasons is $12,000 a season against $3,900 of 2026 gross profit per robot-season, so every paid 2026 robot-season is cash-negative before any fleet-level overhead. The thesis therefore turns entirely on the unbuilt v4 BOM ($31,000-$33,000) and a 74% price increase to $16,500, neither of which has a signed contract behind it. Pick-rate evidence is company-measured on self-selected rows with material exclusions, one pilot has already been lost to bruising, and the technical core sits in a university licence that goes non-exclusive below 100 robots in commercial service on 2027-12-31 (Orrin plans 360). At $72M pre-money for $1.9M FY2025 revenue and $2.6M FY2026E, we are inclined to wait for milestone — a third-party-verified 2027 season of 150+ robots at unit cost below $36,000 with renewals at the $12,700 price — rather than lead at this price, unless the diligence establishes the v4 cost path and customer concentration is cured.",
 "author": "Lead Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-30T01:05:32+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

