{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"red_team_review (by Red Team Analyst):
{
 "answers": {
  "most_damaging_fact": "The v4 unit cost. It is the only variable that turns a loss-making lease book into a viable one, and the one input with no independent evidence: $33,000 appears once, in an August 2026 bill-of-materials quote from a counterparty that is also the $2.5M noteholder, the $1.2M-a-year customer, the board observer and a $1.0M investor. At $44,000 the $16,500 list price stops covering depreciation and no price in the deck works; at $31,000-33,000 on two independent quotes contribution turns positive at list (+$2,650-3,150 a season). Second: the $3.7M of cash and the $1.5M covenant, both unaudited, put the floor at 2027-03-10 and make the closing date an investment condition rather than a timing preference [S1, S95].",
  "company_only_claims": "All of the positive claims that carry value: the $33,000 v4 cost and $48,000 v3 cost; the $3.2M ARR and its $1.4M contracted component; 110 robots contracted for 2027 and 50 letters of intent; 75 paid robot-seasons and 5 paying growers; the 94% pick rate on 212 company-chosen rows, 3.8% bruising and 86% uptime; the $650,000 USDA SBIR Phase II; the Calder licence terms including the 100-robot test; the preference terms; and every executive credential in section 2. The 41% gross margin is a definitional choice, not just an estimate: fleet depreciation sits in operating expense. None of these is corroborated by an independent record retrieved in this run.",
  "generic_section": "Section 1 (company_overview) is the weakest: 19 claims resting only on company material, with no verification status, and one distinct source ID. It asserts the three-entity structure, the Kestrel lien, the $11.0M seed and the board composition, all unverified, while the decision turns on whether the Series A buys the parent that holds the patents and licence or the LLC that holds the grower contracts, the fleet and the debt. Section 2 (team) is next: one source ID, every credential company-reported. Both need record-level evidence, not more wording.",
  "unpriced_risk": "Three. First, the California equipment prohibition: section 13 (esg) treats the rule as a pending trailer bill with a 30-40% spotter risk [S84]; AB 187 passed on 2026-08-31 without the Cal/OSHA fix, so California still requires a human driver in agricultural workplaces, the advisory committee does not meet until 2026-12-01, and the binary is a 20-25% probability of -70% to -90% of equity value [S126, S124]. Second, bridge seniority: at the 2027-03-10 covenant floor the only likely lender is the conflicted counterparty, and bridge paper prices ahead of the Series A — a 35% risk the scenario analysis folds into a generic 'round slips'. Third, preference non-equality: a 30% chance the stack is senior or 2x, worth -$3.8M of recovery at a $25M exit, which no scenario carries [S95].",
  "first_challenge": "Show me the note purchase agreement and the seed certificate of designation. The entire downside in section 8 (funding_valuation) and in the scenario analysis rests on one deck sentence — that the $36.77M preference is 1x, non-participating and equal in rank across Series A, Series A-1 and Series Seed [S95]. If the 2025 notes carry a 2x or senior preference, the lead's recovery at a $25M exit falls from 0.59x to 0.21x, and at $40M from 0.96x to 0.68x. The second challenge is the build cost: a single quote from a counterparty that is also the customer, noteholder and investor is an offer, not evidence.",
  "downside_survival": "The conclusion (pass) survives and strengthens; the downside model does not. A market 50% smaller caps 2031 revenue near $17.7M and a 3-5x exit at $53-88M, or 1.3-2.2x — capital returned, no venture multiple at a $72M pre-money. Revenue 30% lower takes the 2028 plan to $9.2M and the exit to $28-46M, or 0.7-1.1x. But the 0.6-1.0x bear is too generous: at a $25M exit the lead recovers 0.59x on deck terms, 0.21x if the notes are 2x senior, 0.31x behind a $10M senior 2028 round and 0.05x if a pay-to-play converts the Series A; the 1.0x floor is a $39.4-41.6M exit, not $25M. With financing six months late the covenant is breached on 2027-03-10, the 300-unit build misses the April season, the licence test fails below 100 robots on 2027-12-31 and the 2028 round is down: the position returns about its capital on the plan's own probabilities and less once structure risk is priced [S95, S123].",
  "ocr_claims": "None. No OCR-derived claims are used: the deck was read as text, and every figure cited from it is labelled company-provided and unverified. The absence of registry, grant, patent-prosecution and audit records is reported as a confidence ceiling, not as evidence of misrepresentation."
